gcJitT -- SEQ 588 (GC stroke 1, Tony, 2026-11-10): a JIT rung that compiles, collects, and fires from the cache.

After `jitter;` gjCount compiles at its first call into gJitCache, and every later call fires the cached function.
Between the compile and the later calls, collect() runs one collection. The iterate bakes a node minted at emit time
(jitEmitIterStep's iterNode) -- the class of baked address docs/gcRecon.md 3a names the real hazard, because once
LLVM drops the IR module nothing the collector scans holds it.

GJ-1  first call: three members walked, gjAcc 2.
GJ-2  objects the collection found unreachable: 0 today (nothing is collectable). Run under GCSTRESS=0 (poison armed,
      no forced collections); on the branch (stroke 2) it moves.
GJ-3  two cached calls after the collection: gjN 9, gjAcc 9. Today this holds because nothing is collectable. On the
      branch, without R3's per-slot pin list, a freed iterNode would be read by the cached code -- under GCSTRESS's
      poison that faults; with the pin list it holds 9 / 9 again. That is the row R3's H7 reads.

pop.sh also reads the jitter's own exit line (gjCount built 1 called 3), so the row cannot pass on interpreted calls.
